<!-- SECTION 1: Definition (THE ANCHOR) -->
        <p><strong>Western fine art photography</strong> is the photographic expression of <a href="/Blog/what-is-western-fine-art" style="color:#8b5a2b; text-decoration:underline;">Western fine art</a>—authored work engaging the American West with intentional composition, narrative depth, and artistic restraint.</p>

        <p>It is photography created as fine art, not as documentation, décor, or commercial stock imagery. It is defined by authorship: decisions about structure, light, atmosphere, and emotional meaning that elevate the work beyond record-keeping.</p>

        <p>The distinction is not subtle.<br>
        A photograph of a cowboy is not automatically Western fine art.<br>
        A landscape of Monument Valley is not fine art simply because it is beautiful.</p>

        <p>Fine art requires the photographer to make decisions about what the image will mean. The subject does not confer status. The authorship does.</p>

        <p>This article defines what Western fine art photography is, what it is not, and why the distinction matters for collectors, curators, and anyone seeking work that endures on a wall rather than in a folder.</p>

        <!-- EARLY FUNNEL -->
        <div class="early-funnel" style="background:#f8f6f3; border-left:4px solid #8b5a2b; padding:1.25rem 1.5rem; margin:2rem 0; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">
          <p style="margin:0; font-size:1.05rem;">
            The definition matters because the work is meant to be seen, not just explained. Explore the primary
            <a href="/Western-Fine-Art-Photography" style="color:#8b5a2b; font-weight:600; text-decoration:underline;">Western Fine Art Photography collection</a>
            to see how authorship, atmosphere, and restraint carry through the images themselves.
          </p>
          <p style="margin:0.75rem 0 0; font-size:1.05rem;">
            For the artist authority page behind that work, see
